# signers -- the ssh public key every commit in this tree is signed with, in
# openssh's allowed_signers format. this is a PIN, not an identity: the
# principal below is a string in a file in this repo, and so is the fingerprint
# in build.sh. what a pin buys is continuity -- the key cannot be replaced
# except by a commit signed with the key it replaces. see SOURCES.md, "this
# tree's own commits", for what that does and does not prove.
#
# verify a clone with:  ./build.sh vouch
# or by hand:           git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=signers verify-commit HEAD
#
# rotation: add the new line in a commit signed by the OLD key, then remove the
# old line in a commit signed by the NEW one. an ssh signature has no
# revocation certificate -- the chain of signed commits IS the revocation.
